{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.c4735ac7a1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_compare","group":"syn-c-394","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Lake Halden is in eastern Poland. It was first surveyed in 1911.</li><li>Its surface area is 854 square kilometers.</li><li>Lake Dunmore is in central Spain. It was first surveyed in 1978.</li><li>Its surface area is 658 square kilometers.</li><li>Both are reservoirs created by dams.</li></ul><p>The student wants to emphasize how the surface area of the two lakes differs.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Lake Halden was first surveyed in 1911, while Lake Dunmore was first surveyed in 1978.","The surface area of Lake Halden (854 square kilometers) is greater than that of Lake Dunmore (658 square kilometers).","Lake Halden, which is in eastern Poland, is one of several reservoirs created by dams.","Both Lake Halden and Lake Dunmore are reservoirs created by dams."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal asks for a specific kind of comparison. It compares the surface area of both lakes. The other choices describe a similarity, a difference in date, or only one lake."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b3788dcf4e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-2","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Rahman is a marine biologist.</li><li>Rahman wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Rahman recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Rahman found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Rahman concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Rahman recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Rahman concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","Because whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often, Rahman concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","Rahman found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.21dc77a4b5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-3","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Moreau is a marine biologist.</li><li>Moreau wanted to find out whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.</li><li>Moreau recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.</li><li>Moreau found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often.</li><li>Moreau concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Moreau concluded that ship noise may interfere with whale communication.","To investigate the question, Moreau recorded whale songs in a busy shipping lane and in a quiet bay for two months.","Marine biologist Chen Moreau set out to learn whether noise from passing ships affects how often humpback whales sing.","Moreau found that whales in the shipping lane sang about 40 percent less often."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.98692af1e6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-13","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Kofi Castellano is a psychologist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Castellano had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Castellano found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Castellano concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Psychologist Kofi Castellano set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.","To investigate the question, Castellano had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Castellano found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","Castellano concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0528f3903d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-14","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Novak is a psychologist.</li><li>Novak wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Novak had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Novak concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","Novak found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","Psychologist Elias Novak set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6fd3906f1c","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-15","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Oyelaran is a psychologist.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions.</li><li>Oyelaran had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.</li><li>Oyelaran found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Oyelaran concluded that brief breaks may help people stay focused.","To investigate the question, Oyelaran had one group of students take a five-minute break every half hour while another group studied without breaks.","Oyelaran found that students who took breaks answered more practice questions correctly.","Psychologist Jonas Oyelaran set out to learn whether taking short breaks improves students' focus during long study sessions."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.9aa541c7b8","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-18","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Diego Haddad is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Haddad wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Haddad grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Haddad found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Haddad concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Agricultural scientist Diego Haddad set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Haddad concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","To investigate the question, Haddad grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Haddad found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.6090508acf","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-23","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Nadia Nakamura is an agricultural scientist.</li><li>Nakamura wanted to find out whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.</li><li>Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer.</li><li>Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Nakamura grew corn with and without clover planted between the rows for three seasons.","Agricultural scientist Nadia Nakamura set out to learn whether planting clover between rows of corn reduces the need for fertilizer.","Nakamura concluded that clover may supply some of the nutrients corn needs.","Nakamura found that fields with clover produced similar yields using a third less fertilizer."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.930429450d","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-36","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Leila Asante is a sociologist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether community gardens increase how often neighbors talk to one another.</li><li>Asante surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.</li><li>Asante found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.</li><li>Asante concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Asante found that residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened.","To investigate the question, Asante surveyed residents before and one year after gardens opened in their neighborhoods.","Asante concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties.","Because residents reported speaking with neighbors more often after the gardens opened, Asante concluded that community gardens may strengthen neighborhood ties."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.963157426e","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-42","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Chen Kowalski is a geologist.</li><li>Kowalski wanted to find out whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.</li><li>Kowalski measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses.</li><li>Kowalski found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.</li><li>Kowalski concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Kowalski found that bare slopes lost several times more soil.","Kowalski concluded that plant cover may protect hillsides from erosion.","Geologist Chen Kowalski set out to learn whether a hillside's plant cover affects how much soil washes away in heavy rain.","To investigate the question, Kowalski measured soil runoff on bare slopes and on slopes covered with native grasses."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.3a1b565386","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-64","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Imani Lindqvist is an astronomer.</li><li>Lindqvist wanted to find out whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule.</li><li>Lindqvist measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.</li><li>Lindqvist found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.</li><li>Lindqvist concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Lindqvist measured the star's brightness every night for eighteen months.","Lindqvist found that the star dimmed slightly about every 42 days.","Lindqvist concluded that an orbiting planet may be passing in front of the star.","Astronomer Imani Lindqvist set out to learn whether a distant star's brightness changes on a regular schedule."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.bc4ff5e4b6","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-73","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Vance is an economist.</li><li>Vance wanted to find out whether a four-day workweek changes employee productivity.</li><li>Vance tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.</li><li>Vance found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.</li><li>Vance concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Vance found that output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly.","To investigate the question, Vance tracked output at companies before and after they adopted a four-day week.","Vance concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity.","Because output per employee stayed the same or rose slightly, Vance concluded that shorter workweeks may not reduce productivity."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.894b15c5bd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-86","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Idris Oyelaran is an urban planner.</li><li>Oyelaran wanted to find out whether adding trees along streets lowers summer temperatures.</li><li>Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover.</li><li>Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.</li><li>Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Because streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler, Oyelaran concluded that street trees may help cities stay cooler in summer.","Oyelaran found that streets with trees were up to four degrees cooler.","To investigate the question, Oyelaran measured afternoon temperatures on streets with and without tree cover."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.736c5a7ef5","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-93","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Sato is a hydrologist.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.</li><li>Sato compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.</li><li>Sato found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.</li><li>Sato concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Sato found that river levels rose more slowly below the wetlands.","To investigate the question, Sato compared river levels below restored wetlands with levels below drained farmland after storms.","Hydrologist Elias Sato set out to learn whether wetlands reduce flooding downstream.","Sato concluded that wetlands may help protect nearby communities from floods."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.843ddd2a03","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-97","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Sofia Quintero is an education researcher.</li><li>Quintero wanted to find out whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.</li><li>Quintero had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.</li><li>Quintero found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words.</li><li>Quintero concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["To investigate the question, Quintero had parents in one group read aloud daily while another group continued their usual routines.","Education researcher Sofia Quintero set out to learn whether reading aloud to young children improves their vocabulary.","Quintero concluded that regular read-aloud time may support vocabulary growth.","Quintero found that children in the read-aloud group learned more new words."],"answer":"D","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.b6acd459ab","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-125","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Felipe Okafor is an ornithologist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether birds that migrate farther have longer wings.</li><li>Okafor measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.</li><li>Okafor found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.</li><li>Okafor concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor found that species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings.","Okafor concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance.","To investigate the question, Okafor measured the wings of several related species with different migration distances.","Because species that migrated farther tended to have longer, narrower wings, Okafor concluded that wing shape may be linked to migration distance."],"answer":"D","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.a3068f03a1","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-130","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Dmitri Iyer is a public health researcher.</li><li>Iyer wanted to find out whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle.</li><li>Iyer counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.</li><li>Iyer found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.</li><li>Iyer concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Iyer concluded that bike lanes may encourage more people to ride.","To investigate the question, Iyer counted cyclists on streets before and after new bike lanes were added.","Iyer found that cycling on those streets nearly doubled within a year.","Public health researcher Dmitri Iyer set out to learn whether adding bike lanes increases how often residents cycle."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7491a69fb9","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-141","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Sato is a neuroscientist.</li><li>Sato wanted to find out whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.</li><li>Sato scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.</li><li>Sato found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.</li><li>Sato concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.</li></ul><p>The student wants to describe how the study was conducted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Neuroscientist Ravi Sato set out to learn whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.","To investigate the question, Sato scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.","Sato concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.","Sato found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It explains the procedure. The others give the study's aim, finding, or conclusion."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.ca27427e67","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-143","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Tomás Castellano is a neuroscientist.</li><li>Castellano wanted to find out whether learning to juggle changes brain structure.</li><li>Castellano scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.</li><li>Castellano found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.</li><li>Castellano concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Castellano found that regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger.","To investigate the question, Castellano scanned volunteers' brains before and after three months of juggling practice.","Because regions linked to tracking movement grew slightly larger, Castellano concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain.","Castellano concluded that practicing a new skill may reshape parts of the brain."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.79fdf95bb4","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-155","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Ravi Okafor is a climate scientist.</li><li>Okafor wanted to find out whether light-colored roofs reduce indoor temperatures.</li><li>Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.</li><li>Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler.</li><li>Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","Because homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler, Okafor concluded that light-colored roofs may reduce the need for air-conditioning.","To investigate the question, Okafor compared indoor temperatures in homes with white roofs and homes with dark roofs.","Okafor found that homes with white roofs stayed several degrees cooler."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.7ca9aceebd","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-156","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Elias Takeda is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Takeda wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","To investigate the question, Takeda moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Because fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays, Takeda concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","Takeda found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.1e4718eeeb","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-157","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Yara Mbeki is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Mbeki wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Mbeki moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Mbeki found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Mbeki concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present the study's finding.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Mbeki found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","Behavioral economist Yara Mbeki set out to learn whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.","To investigate the question, Mbeki moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Mbeki concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices."],"answer":"A","explanation":"It reports what the researcher observed. A conclusion is an interpretation of a finding, not the finding itself."},
{"id":"g.rw.synthesis.2.0c33f1a15b","section":"rw","domain":"expression","skill":"synthesis","difficulty":2,"tmpl":"rw_synthesis.build_studies","group":"syn-s-160","passage":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p><ul><li>Jonas Asante is a behavioral economist.</li><li>Asante wanted to find out whether shoppers buy more fruit when it is placed near the store entrance.</li><li>Asante moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.</li><li>Asante found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.</li><li>Asante concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.</li></ul><p>The student wants to present both the study's finding and the conclusion drawn from it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Asante found that fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays.","Because fruit sales rose in the stores with entrance displays, Asante concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices.","To investigate the question, Asante moved the fruit display to the entrance in some stores but not others.","Asante concluded that product placement may influence shoppers' choices."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The goal has two parts, and only this choice includes both the finding and the conclusion. The others give only one part, or the method."}
